Devon Weckherlen & Daniel Mihovch

Raspberry Pi Rubik's Cube

How to Use:

  Starting:
    Lay Pi on flat surface to calibrate
    After calibration, the top face of the cube will be displayed as a 6x6 gird on the Pi
      The 6x6 displayed face represents the TOP of the cube.
      Rotating the cube will change the relative top of the cube to whatever side it is being rotated to.

  Using:
    There will be a SELECT mode and an ACTION mode.
      SELECT MODE:
        You are in SELECT mode when the outer most colors of the flat view are CYAN.
        Outer ring of colors represents what row and column you are in.
        Use joystick left, right, up, and down to select which row and/or column you want to act on.
      ACTION MODE:
        You are in ACTION mode when the outer most colors of the flat view are PURPLE.
        Based on what row and column you are hovering over from select mode.
        If you use the joystick up, it will rotate whatever column you have selected up.
        If you use the joystick right, it will rotate whatever row you have selected to the right.
        This mimics the action of rotating sides on a Rubik's Cube.
    
    While in SELECT mode and leaning the Pi to a cardinal direction, It will display the TOP view of
    the Pi and whatever side you are leaning it to's side in 2 separate 3x3 grids
      Ex: You are in select mode and lean the cube to the RIGHT. The TOP view will be displayed on
      the LEFT, and the RIGHT side will be displayed on the RIGHT.
      The appearance of the tilted side will display as it should relative to the top side. (See diagram)

    While in SELECT mode and leaning the Pi to a cardinal direction and pushing the joystick in that 
    corresponding cardinal direction, the pi view will be rotated to now display that side as the new
    top side.
      Ex: You are in select mode and lean the cube to the RIGHT and press the joystick to the RIGHT.
      The Pi will scroll to the RIGHT and become the new TOP side.
      The appearance of the scrolled to side will be the same as it was in the preview

Visual Diagram:

           |----------|
           |          |
           |    UP    |
           |          |
|----------|----------|----------|
|          |          |          |
|   LEFT   |   TOP    |   RIGHT  |
|          |          |          |
|----------|----------|----------|
           |          |
           |   DOWN   |
           |          |
           |----------|
           |          |
           |  BOTTOM  |
           |          |
           |----------|

- Imagine you are always looking at the top side as the main view and everything else
  appears relative to the top side
- Imagine all the sides fold in to form the cube shape


NOTES:
  - When "rotating the cube" the pi screen should act as a scrolling display and scroll to that side 
    - If the side being scrolled to is up, the visual is scrolling down to that up side
  - When "rotating a side" it should also scroll that specific row or column
  - should implement a "scramble" command that you can type in the command line to scramble the cube
  - If we wanted to use Dans virtual Pi simulator, we could make it so arrow key movements correspond
    to Pi gyro movements (tilt_left = arrow_left)
  - I just realized something about cube rotations. when you are holding the pi and tilting it to the left,
    you are doing that because you want to view the right side. But if I'm tilting the pi to the left, I
    will be pushing the joystick to the right because that is the side im viewing. This means the cube
    rotation should happen when the tilt and joystick values are opposite

File Structure:
- main.c
  - Main while loop for running the cube.
    - in the while loop there are a bunch of if statements for stuff like:
      if (xRoll == right) {
        display_right;
        if (joystick == right) {
          rotate_right;
        }
      }
  - Constantly checking for updates from the gyroscopes and joystick
  - Struct called "Cube" to represent the visual diagram of the cube with the fields:
    - top: int[][]
    - bottom: int[][]
    - left: int[][]
    - right: int[][]
    - up: int[][]
    - down: int[][]
  - Scramble function that reads input from the command line and calls the scramble function
  - Mainly uses functions from display.c but also from scramble.c

- display.c
  - Handles all display functionality with viewing sides, scroll rotating and individual
    row/column scroll rotatation
  - Have definitions of numbers that correspond to colors
    - white = 0
    - yellow = 1
    - red = 2
    - orange = 3
    - green = 4
    - blue = 5

    SEMI-IMPORTANT FUN FACT: White is always opposite yellow, red is always opposite orange, 
    and green is always opposite blue. If white is the TOP face, and red is the UP face, the 
    LEFT face will always be blue, the RIGHT face will alawys be green, the DOWN face will 
    alawys be orange, and the BOTTOM face will always be yellow
    ADDITIONALLY, this is true for even a scrambled cube. The center dots of a cube still follow
    this rule, which could be key to debugging this to make sure things actually work as intended

  - Each field of the cube will be a 2D array of numbers that correspond to the colors
  - display fucnctions will mainly use map.c for the backend rotation logic

- map.c
  - Handles mapping of rubiks cube values after cube rotations and side rotations 
    - Definitions:
      - Cube Rotation: When the whole face of the cube is rotated to a new side (tilt + joystick)
      - Side Rotation: When a singular row or column of the cube is rotatated (Action mode + joystick)
  - Function called "remap" will be called that handle cube rotations and will remap the whole cube 
    so that the new side becomes the new top side and all other sides rotate accordingly
  - Functions for handinle the maping of side rotations
    - Ex:
    - middle_up: middle column of the cube rotate up
    - middle_left: middle row of the cube rotate left
    - left_down: left column of the cube rotate down
    - up_left: top row of the cube rotate left
    - down_right: bottom row of the cube rotate right

- cube.c
  - initalize cubes state
  - When user enters "scramble" into the command line, it will scramble the cube by randomly calling
    the functions from map.c 100 times.
    - Remap with a random cardinal direction is in the pool of random functions to be called for 
      maximum scramblage
  - When user enters "scramble [int]" into the command line, it will scramble the cube [int] times.
    Works the same as the regular scramble function otherwise
  - This scramble happens when you are about to run the executable File




  